
Theoretical Foundations of a Hand–Forearm Perceptual Module with Distributed Perception

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta AI


Abstract

Robotic manipulation under occlusion remains fundamentally limited by the spatial concentration of perception: when an external camera loses line of sight, the contact state, local object geometry, and friction parameters become unobservable from proprioception and wrist force/torque alone. This thesis argues that the spatial distribution of heterogeneous sensors along the hand–forearm mechanical chain is not merely an engineering convenience but a structural determinant of observability. We formalize a hand–forearm perceptual module in which the forearm is simultaneously actuator, tendon transmission, and internal perceptual organ, and in which the wrist functions as an explicit fusion node between arm-side and hand-side inference. The state space is defined as \mathcal{X} = \mathcal{Q} \times T\mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{L} \times \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{W}, with contact state c = (p, n, f, \mu) \in \mathbb{R}^3 \times S^2 \times \mathbb{R}^3 \times \mathbb{R}_+ of dimension 9 per contact point, and tendon length L included to capture configuration-dependent compliance and Bowden-cable hysteresis. Estimation is formulated as inference on a factor graph with dynamics, proprioceptive, tactile, visual, and internal perceptual factors, where the wrist separator induces conditional independence between hand and forearm subgraphs. The central theoretical contribution is a proposition proving that under external occlusion, there exists an open set \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}} \subset \mathcal{X} on which the observability codistribution of the distributed architecture has strictly higher rank than that of a centralized architecture with equivalent sensor count, with rank increase bounded below by \dim(\mathcal{C}) - \dim(\mathcal{W}_{\text{FT}}) \geq 2 for two or more contact points. This is complemented by an information-theoretic formulation showing that the architectural information gain \Delta I_{\text{occ}} = I(w; S_{\text{dist}} \mid M=0) - I(w; S_{\text{ext}} \mid M=0) is strictly positive under occlusion, and by identifiability conditions establishing that heterogeneous tactile sensing — GelSight for geometry (p, n), TacTip for shear and friction (f, \mu) — is necessary rather than preferential for injectivity of c \mapsto (z_T, z_V, z_Q). The internal forearm camera observes tendon deformation \delta = \Psi(\tau, q, L) directly, providing an independent estimate of tendon elongation that compensates for friction-induced hysteresis and underactuation null space. A V0.1 technical specification is provided with explicit DoF, motor, tendon, sensor, bus bandwidth, thermal, and latency budgets, including a 12.96 Gbps camera aggregate requiring FPGA-based preprocessing and a 21 ms sensor-to-estimate pipeline compatible with 50 ms slip detection. An experimental protocol with statistical power analysis, calibration drift measurement over 3300 grasps, thermal drift characterization, and factor graph ablations isolates the architectural effect from sensor-level effects. The falsifiable prediction is that the prototype outperforms an Allegro V5 Plus baseline with equivalent tactile instrumentation by more than 20\% on occlusion-conditioned localization error and slip detection latency at equal mass, and that ablation of internal perception produces the largest rank deficiency and the largest performance drop under occlusion.

1. Problem Statement and Formal Framework

The central problem is the estimation of the world state \hat{W}(t) from heterogeneous measurements distributed over a hand–forearm system. We seek to construct an estimator

\hat{W}(t) = f\bigl(S(t),\, u(t),\, \theta\bigr),

where S(t) is the set of measurements, u(t) the motor command, and \theta the calibrated parameters. The thesis defended here is that the spatial structure of S(t) — the distribution of sensors along the mechanical chain — determines an observability property that a centralized architecture with an equivalent number of sensors does not possess.

1.1 State Space

Let \mathcal{X} be the state space:

\mathcal{X} = \mathcal{Q} \times T\mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{L} \times \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{W},

with:

· \mathcal{Q} \subset \mathbb{R}^n: joint configuration space, n active degrees of freedom;
· T\mathcal{Q} \cong \mathbb{R}^n: tangent space of joint velocities;
· \mathcal{L} \subset \mathbb{R}^m: tendon length space, m tendons, capturing configuration-dependent elongation;
· \mathcal{T} \subset \mathbb{R}^m: tendon tension space;
· \mathcal{C}: contact manifold;
· \mathcal{W}: world state space (pose, local geometry, texture).

The contact state at a single point is defined as

c = (p, n, f, \mu) \in \mathbb{R}^3 \times S^2 \times \mathbb{R}^3 \times \mathbb{R}_+,

so that \dim c = 3 + 2 + 3 + 1 = 9. This corrects the earlier dimensional inconsistency: with k simultaneous contact points, \dim \mathcal{C} = 9k, and the effective dimension is

\dim \mathcal{X} = 2n + 2m + 9k + \dim \mathcal{W}.

The complete state is x = (q, \dot{q}, L, \tau, c, w) \in \mathcal{X}.

The inclusion of tendon length L as a state variable is essential: in a Bowden-cable transmission routed through the forearm and wrist, tension \tau is not a static function of motor torque. Configuration-dependent elongation, sheath friction, and hysteresis introduce a nonlinear, configuration-dependent relationship between motor-side and joint-side variables. The map from motor torque to joint torque is mediated by the coupling matrix C^T(q) \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}, and without modeling compliance, the internal observation model is structurally incorrect.

1.2 Measurement Model

Each sensor i \in \{1, \dots, N\} produces a measurement

z_i = h_i(x) + v_i, \qquad v_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R_i(t, T)),

where h_i : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_i} is the observation model and R_i(t, T) \succ 0 is a time- and temperature-dependent noise covariance. This replaces the earlier constant-covariance assumption, which is invalid for vision-based tactile sensors. The GelSlim calibration framework explicitly demonstrates that sensor output degrades over time: after approximately 1700 grasps, signal strength begins to decay, and online brightness and contrast adjustment are required to maintain consistency over 3300 grasps. Frictional wear produces dead pixels and localized signal loss. The covariance must therefore be modeled as

R_i(t, T) = R_i^{(0)} + \Sigma_{\text{wear}}(n_g) + \Sigma_{\text{thermal}}(T),

where n_g is the cumulative grasp count. This is not a minor calibration detail; it is a structural requirement for long-horizon identifiability.

The sensors are partitioned into four families:

\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{S}_{\text{prop}} \cup \mathcal{S}_{\text{tact}} \cup \mathcal{S}_{\text{vis}} \cup \mathcal{S}_{\text{int}},

where \mathcal{S}_{\text{prop}} provides proprioception (q, \dot{q}), \mathcal{S}_{\text{tact}} provides contact and force (c, w), \mathcal{S}_{\text{vis}} provides external and on-hand visual observations, and \mathcal{S}_{\text{int}} provides internal mechanical state (L, \tau, P) via cable tension, motor current, and the internal forearm camera.

The specificity of the proposed architecture is that \mathcal{S}_{\text{int}} is a perceptual modality in its own right. The low gear ratios used in the 1X NEO hand — approximately 5:1 to 15:1, compared with 100:1 or 200:1 in industrial systems — create “force transparency” that allows contact forces to pass back through the transmission, enabling each joint to function simultaneously as a motor and a sensor. The forearm camera observes the deformation of springs and tendons directly, making the transmission itself an exteroceptive channel.

