2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Probabilistic Graphical Representation

The estimation problem is formulated as inference in a factor graph \mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{F}), following the Contact Factor Graph (CFG) formulation that has been developed for simultaneous tactile estimation and control of extrinsic contact. The variables \mathcal{V} contain the discretized state over a sliding window:

\mathcal{V} = \{x_t, x_{t-1}, \dots, x_{t-L}\}.

The factors \mathcal{F} encode the measurement and dynamics models:

\mathcal{F} = \mathcal{F}_{\text{dyn}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{prop}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{tact}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{vis}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{int}}.

Dynamics factor. The joint and tendon dynamics are

\mathcal{F}_{\text{dyn}}:\quad q_{t+1} = f(q_t, L_t, \tau_t) + w_t, \qquad w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q),

with the tendon length dynamics

L_{t+1} = L_t + \Delta L(\tau_t, q_t) + w_t^L.

Proprioceptive factor. Encoders and IMU provide

\mathcal{F}_{\text{prop}}:\quad z_Q = q_t + v_Q, \qquad v_Q \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R_Q(t,T)).

Internal perceptual factor. The forearm camera observes the tendon/spring bundle:

\mathcal{F}_{\text{int}}:\quad z_P = \Phi \circ \Psi(\tau_t, q_t, L_t) + v_P.

This factor is the mathematical embodiment of the claim that the forearm is a perceptual organ.

Tactile factor. Following the factor-graph tactile modeling literature, local image measurements at the point of contact are used to predict relative poses, which are then incorporated as factors:

\mathcal{F}_{\text{tact}}:\quad z_T = h_T(c_t, w_t) + v_T.

Visual factor. External and on-hand cameras provide

\mathcal{F}_{\text{vis}}:\quad z_V = h_V(w_t, q_t) \cdot M_{\text{vis}}(q_t, w_t) + v_V,

where M_{\text{vis}} is the occlusion mask.

The MAP estimate is

\hat{\mathcal{V}} = \arg\max_{\mathcal{V}} \prod_{f_i \in \mathcal{F}} f_i(\mathcal{V}_i),

and for Gaussian factors

f_i(\mathcal{V}_i) \propto \exp\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\bigl\|h_i(\mathcal{V}_i) - z_i\bigr\|_{R_i^{-1}}^2\right).

2.2 Wrist as Separator Node

The wrist plays the role of a separator node between the arm subgraph and the hand subgraph. This is made explicit by conditional independence:

p(\mathcal{X}_A \mid \mathcal{X}_B, z_{\text{wrist}}) = p(\mathcal{X}_A \mid z_{\text{wrist}}) \cdot p(\mathcal{X}_B \mid z_{\text{wrist}}),

when the wrist F/T sensor is measured. This decomposition isolates the hand-and-fingers innovation \mathcal{X}_A from the forearm infrastructure \mathcal{X}_B, with \mathcal{X}_C (shoulder) standardized and without a proper innovation factor in V0.

2.3 Tendon Elasticity and the Forearm Map

Let \Psi : \mathbb{R}^m \times \mathcal{Q} \times \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathcal{D} be the map that, given a tension configuration \tau, joint positions q, and tendon lengths L, associates the deformation field \delta \in \mathcal{D} of the springs and tendons in the forearm:

\delta = \Psi(\tau, q, L).

The internal camera observes

z_P = \Phi(\delta) + v_P = \Phi \circ \Psi(\tau, q, L) + v_P,

where \Phi is the observation operator. Linearizing around (\tau_0, q_0, L_0):

z_P \approx \Phi(\delta_0) + J_\Phi J_\Psi
\begin{bmatrix}
\Delta \tau \\
\Delta q \\
\Delta L
\end{bmatrix}
+ v_P,

with J_\Psi = \partial \Psi / \partial (\tau, q, L) and J_\Phi = \partial \Phi / \partial \delta. This yields a direct measurement of a linear combination of tendon tensions, joint positions, and tendon elongation. The key observation is that the forearm camera estimates \Delta L directly, enabling compensation for the configuration-dependent compliance that would otherwise degrade joint torque estimation. This turns the transmission nonlinearity from a liability into a sensed quantity.

2.4 Heterogeneous Tactile Sensing: GelSight and TacTip

The tactile sensing requirements are heterogeneous: geometry reconstruction and shear/slip detection impose different constraints on the sensing modality.

GelSight360 is a fingertip-like, omnidirectional, camera-based tactile sensor capable of producing depth maps of objects deforming the sensor's surface. It measures normal strain via photometric stereo and provides high-resolution local geometry (p, n).

TacTip is a soft optical tactile sensor with biomimetic morphology, transducing deformation via internal pins inspired by dermal papillae. The recovered force distribution from marker motions enables shear analysis, contact force estimation, and slip detection. A modified TacTip with biomimetic fingerprint ridges has been shown to induce and detect incipient slip prior to gross slip onset. It provides tangential force and friction information (f, \mu).

For identifiability of c = (p, n, f, \mu), both modalities are required. We therefore propose a heterogeneous finger configuration: thumb and index with GelSight Svelte (geometry), middle, ring, and little with TacTip (shear and slip). This makes the injectivity claim of the map c \mapsto (z_T, z_V, z_Q) provable, rather than assumed.

