3. Observability and Identifiability

3.1 Discrete-Time Observability

Consider the nonlinear discrete-time system

x_{t+1} = f(x_t, u_t), \qquad z_t = h(x_t) + v_t.

The local observability matrix over a window of length L is

\mathcal{O}(x_{t-L:t}) =
\begin{bmatrix}
\partial h/\partial x \big|_{x_t} \\
\partial h/\partial x \big|_{x_{t+1}} \partial f/\partial x \big|_{x_t} \\
\vdots \\
\partial h/\partial x \big|_{x_{t-L}} \prod_{k=0}^{L-1} \partial f/\partial x \big|_{x_{t-k}}
\end{bmatrix}.

The system is locally observable at x_0 if

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}(x_0) = \dim \mathcal{X}.

3.2 Proposition (Distributed Perception Strictly Enlarges the Observable Subspace under Occlusion)

Proposition. Let \mathcal{A}_{\text{ext}} be an architecture with a single external camera and proprioception, and \mathcal{A}_{\text{dist}} the proposed distributed architecture. Under partial occlusion of the object by the fingers, there exists an open set \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}} \subset \mathcal{X} such that for all x \in \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}},

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x) < \dim \mathcal{X}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{dist}}(x) = \dim \mathcal{X}.

Proof sketch. Consider an object held such that the external camera observes only the dorsal side of the fingers. Define the occlusion mask

M_{\text{vis}}(q, w) =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{if the object surface is occluded by the hand}, \\
1 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Under M_{\text{vis}} = 0, the visual measurement provides no constraint on the contact geometry c or local object geometry w:

\partial h_{\text{vis, ext}} / \partial (c, w) \approx 0.

In \mathcal{A}_{\text{ext}}, the only remaining measurements are proprioception z_Q = q and, possibly, wrist F/T. The map from (q, \tau) to (c, w) is rank-deficient: multiple contact configurations can produce the same joint torques. Specifically, for any two contact configurations c_1 \neq c_2 satisfying

C^T(q) \tau(c_1, w) = C^T(q) \tau(c_2, w),

the proprioceptive and wrist F/T rows of \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}} are identical, and \operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}} < \dim \mathcal{X}.

In \mathcal{A}_{\text{dist}}, the palm cameras observe the inter-finger space, the finger tactile sensors observe the contact patch, and the internal forearm camera constrains tendon elongation and tension. The combined observation map

h_{\text{dist}} = (h_{\text{prop}}, h_{\text{tact}}, h_{\text{vis, palm}}, h_{\text{vis, finger}}, h_{\text{int}})

has a Jacobian whose rank exceeds that of h_{\text{ext}} at the occluded configuration. The palm and finger tactile rows add independent constraints on (c, w) that are not spanned by the proprioceptive rows. The internal forearm row adds an independent constraint on (L, \tau), which further constrains the configuration. Therefore,

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{dist}}(x) > \operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x)

for x \in \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}}, and the unobservable subspace of \mathcal{A}_{\text{dist}} is strictly smaller. \square

3.3 Information-Theoretic Formulation

The rank argument is qualitative. A stronger, quantitative statement uses mutual information. Under occlusion, the external visual channel provides negligible information about the object state:

I(w; z_{\text{vis, ext}} \mid q) \approx 0.

The distributed architecture provides positive information even when the external camera is fully occluded, because the palm and tactile channels are physically coupled to the contact state:

I(w; S_{\text{tact}}, S_{\text{vis, palm}} \mid q, \tau) > 0.

Recent empirical evidence supports this claim: simultaneous tactile-visual perception achieves an average success rate of 85.5% on five challenging manipulation tasks, compared with 55.4% for vision-only policies, with particular advantage in contact detection with thin and soft objects where visual occlusion is severe. The distributed architecture is designed to realize this gap by construction, rather than relying on a learned policy to exploit incidental tactile signals.

3.4 Friction Identifiability Requires Shear Sensing

The friction coefficient \mu is not identifiable from a static normal pressure map alone. Identifiability of \mu requires either:

1. shear-sensitive tactile sensing, which TacTip provides through lateral marker displacement;
2. active slip or incipient-slip excitation, which produces observable tangential displacement;
3. tangential force/torque measurements from the finger F/T sensors.

The heterogeneous finger configuration — GelSight for geometry, TacTip for shear — is therefore not a design preference but an identifiability requirement.

