4. Architecture and Design

4.1 Three-Level Architecture

Level C — Shoulder (standardized interface). ISO 9409-1-50 flange, 48 V power, EtherCAT. No shoulder R&D in V0. Mounted on UR10e, Franka, or Kinova.

Level B — Forearm (perceptual mechatronic core). Length 220–240 mm. 13–15 brushless motors (Faulhaber class), ratio 5:1 to 15:1 for force transparency. Tendon transmission: Dyneema 0.4 mm, PTFE tubes, idler pulleys. Internal sensors: cable tension, motor current, temperature, 512 imp/rev encoders, 6-axis IMU. Internal wide-angle camera observing tendon/spring bundle. Computing: 1× Orin Nano + Artix-7 FPGA. The FPGA is required for parallel processing of the camera array, following the KIT Finger-Vision architecture, which uses an FPGA and microcontroller hybrid to process visual data from cameras in each fingertip.

Level A — Hand (active perceptual network).

· Wrist (fusion node): 2 DoF, 6-axis F/T sensor, 120° RGB camera + miniature ToF near palm.
· Palm (anti-occlusion): 2 micro-cameras (2 mm) viewing between fingers, 1 central GelSight Svelte or DIGIT.
· Fingers: per finger, 1 tendon + 1 return spring. Modalities: Q (pose from internal marker tracking), F (1D/3D force sensor at pulp), T_{\text{int}} (TacTip or GelSight360 at distal pulp), V_{\text{ext}} (2 Mpix lateral micro-camera in V0.1b).

4.2 DoF and Motor Mapping

