Total: 17 actuated DoF, 12 motors, 15 tendons. The underactuation per finger (3 joints, 2 motors, 1 tendon with return spring) is consistent with the soft-hand design philosophy but introduces configuration-dependent coupling that the internal forearm camera observes.

4.3 Sensor Placement and Modalities

The placement follows a spatial sampling principle: each sensor covers a region of the state space that is not accessible to the others. The palm cameras cover the inter-digital space; the finger tactile sensors cover the contact patch; the wrist camera covers the pre-shaping workspace; the forearm camera covers the tendon state. The union of their fields of view covers the manipulation workspace with minimal redundancy. The in-finger cameras provide redundant visual information without inevitable occlusion by the gripper itself before contact, and enable pose recovery for the cameras, which in turn allows inferring finger poses without internal sensors.

4.4 Fusion Architecture

For V0, we adopt the factor graph formulation of Section 2.1:

· Variables: joint poses, tendon lengths, tendon tensions, contact, slip, local geometry.
· Factors: encoders, IMU, F/T, tactile, vision, internal camera.
· Real-time optimization, interpretable, clean ablations.

For V1, a lightweight cross-modal transformer can be introduced, where each sensor stream is tokenized and cross-attention is applied between palm, fingers, and wrist. The factor graph provides the baseline and the interpretability.

