6. Experimental Validation Protocol

6.1 Hypothesis

A hand–forearm architecture with distributed perception increases the rank of the observability codistribution under occlusion by at least \dim(\mathcal{C}), and this increase is measurable as I(w; S_{\text{dist}}) - I(w; S_{\text{ext}}) > 0.

6.2 Baseline and Prototype

Baseline A. Allegro V5 Plus 16 DoF + 360° tactile + external RealSense.
Baseline B. EyeSight Hand: 7 DoF, integrated vision-based tactile sensors, quasi-direct drive actuation, designed for whole-hand manipulation.
Prototype C. Our hand–forearm module.

The inclusion of EyeSight Hand as a second baseline is required because it directly benchmarks integrated vision-based tactile sensing with compliant actuation, which is the closest architectural competitor.

6.3 Tasks

· 3D reconstruction of a held object under quantitative occlusion, defined as the fraction of object surface unseen by the external camera.
· Contact/slip detection latency: time from slip onset to \hat{W}(t) update.
· Grasping of an object hidden behind another.
· Fine insertion.
· In-hand manipulation.

6.4 Metrics

Success rate; localization error; reconstruction error; latency S \to \hat{W}; mass; power; heating; robustness to occlusion; calibration drift; thermal drift.

6.5 Failure Modes and Ablations

Calibration drift. Measure reconstruction error before and after 1000 and 3300 grasps, following the GelSlim calibration protocol. The GelSlim framework demonstrates that signal strength degrades after approximately 1700 grasps and that online brightness and contrast adjustment restores consistency over 3300 grasps. The prototype must demonstrate comparable or better stability.

Thermal drift. Measure tactile reconstruction error as a function of forearm temperature over 0–50°C.

Ablations. Factor graph ablations: remove palm cameras, remove finger tactile, remove forearm internal camera, remove all internal perception. Measure rank deficiency and task success rate for each ablation.

6.6 Falsifiable Prediction

Prototype C beats Baseline A by >20% on occlusion-conditioned localization error and slip detection latency at equal mass. If the observability proposition is correct, the ablation removing internal perception should produce the largest rank deficiency and the largest performance drop under occlusion.

