7. Novelty and Contributions

The novelty is not a new sensor, nor a new hand, but a hand–forearm architecture where the forearm is simultaneously actuator, transmission, and internal perceptual organ, and where the wrist is an explicit fusion node between arm vision and hand vision, with distributed anti-occlusion perception.

The mathematical contribution is the formalization of distributed perception as an observability problem on a factor graph, with a proposition proving strict rank increase under occlusion, an information-theoretic quantification of the occlusion gap, and an identifiability analysis that grounds the heterogeneous tactile sensor configuration in necessary conditions rather than design preference.

The technical contribution is the V0.1 specification with explicit DoF/motor/sensor tables, bus bandwidth analysis, thermal budget, and an experimental protocol that isolates the architectural effect from sensor-level effects, including calibration drift, thermal drift, and factor graph ablations as primary failure-mode measurements.
