Appendix B. Full Proof of Proposition 3.2 (Observability Rank Increase under Occlusion)

B.1 Setup

Consider the discrete-time nonlinear system on the window \{t-L, \dots, t\}:

x_{s+1} = f(x_s, u_s), \qquad z_s = h(x_s) + v_s.

The local observability matrix is

\mathcal{O}(x_{t-L:t}) =
\begin{bmatrix}
H_t \\
H_{t-1} F_{t-1} \\
\vdots \\
H_{t-L} \prod_{k=0}^{L-1} F_{t-1-k}
\end{bmatrix},

where H_s = \partial h / \partial x |_{x_s} and F_s = \partial f / \partial x |_{x_s}.

The unobservable subspace at x_0 is

\mathcal{N}(x_0) = \ker \mathcal{O}(x_0).

Local observability holds iff \dim \mathcal{N}(x_0) = 0.

B.2 External Architecture

The external architecture has measurement map

h_{\text{ext}} = (h_{\text{prop}}, h_{\text{FT}}, h_{\text{vis, ext}}),

h_{\text{prop}}(x) = q, \qquad h_{\text{FT}}(x) = g_{\text{FT}}(c, w), \qquad h_{\text{vis, ext}}(x) = g_{\text{vis}}(w, q) \cdot M_{\text{vis}}(q, w).

Jacobian block structure:

H_{\text{ext}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
I_n & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \partial g_{\text{FT}}/\partial c & \partial g_{\text{FT}}/\partial w \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \partial g_{\text{vis}}/\partial w \cdot M_{\text{vis}} + g_{\text{vis}} \cdot \partial M_{\text{vis}}/\partial w
\end{bmatrix}.

B.3 Occlusion Condition

Definition B.1. The object is occluded from the external camera at configuration (q, w) if

M_{\text{vis}}(q, w) = 0.

Under this condition,

\partial h_{\text{vis, ext}} / \partial x = 0

for all components except through g_{\text{vis}} \cdot \partial M_{\text{vis}}/\partial w, which is supported only on the occlusion boundary. In the interior of the occluded region, the visual row contributes zero rank.

B.4 Lemma (Rank Deficiency of the External Architecture)

Lemma B.2. Let x_0 = (q_0, \dot{q}_0, L_0, \tau_0, c_0, w_0) be a state with M_{\text{vis}}(q_0, w_0) = 0 and k \geq 2 simultaneous contact points. Suppose the two contact points (p_1, n_1, f_1, \mu_1) and (p_2, n_2, f_2, \mu_2) satisfy the force-balance constraint

f_1 + f_2 = f_{\text{ext}}, \qquad p_1 \times f_1 + p_2 \times f_2 = \tau_{\text{ext}},

for fixed external wrench (f_{\text{ext}}, \tau_{\text{ext}}). Then

\dim \mathcal{N}_{\text{ext}}(x_0) \geq 2.

Proof. Consider the infinitesimal perturbation

\delta c = (\delta p_1, \delta n_1, \delta f_1, \delta \mu_1, \delta p_2, \delta n_2, \delta f_2, \delta \mu_2),

with

\delta f_1 = -\delta f_2, \qquad \delta p_1 \times f_1 + p_1 \times \delta f_1 + \delta p_2 \times f_2 + p_2 \times \delta f_2 = 0.

This defines a 2-dimensional subspace of contact perturbations that preserve the external wrench. By construction,

\partial g_{\text{FT}} / \partial c \cdot \delta c = 0.

Since M_{\text{vis}} = 0,

\partial h_{\text{vis, ext}} / \partial c \cdot \delta c = 0.

And h_{\text{prop}} depends only on q, so

\partial h_{\text{prop}} / \partial c \cdot \delta c = 0.

Therefore \delta c \in \ker H_{\text{ext}}. Since the dynamics f are independent of c at first order (contact does not enter the joint dynamics directly except through \tau, which is unperturbed), the full observability matrix satisfies

\mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x_0) \cdot \delta c = 0.

Hence \dim \mathcal{N}_{\text{ext}}(x_0) \geq 2. \square

The factor 2 is a lower bound; in general, the null space includes the friction coefficient \mu (unobservable from static normal force) and the contact geometry (p, n) when the tactile channel is absent.

B.5 Distributed Architecture

The distributed architecture adds the measurement rows

h_{\text{tact}}(x) = g_T(c, w), \qquad h_{\text{vis, palm}}(x) = g_{V_p}(c, w), \qquad h_{\text{vis, finger}}(x) = g_{V_f}(c, w), \qquad h_{\text{int}}(x) = \Phi \circ \Psi(\tau, q, L).

Lemma B.3. Under the same occlusion condition M_{\text{vis, ext}} = 0, the tactile and palm rows satisfy

\partial g_T / \partial c \neq 0, \qquad \partial g_{V_p} / \partial c \neq 0

on an open set of contact configurations \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}}.

Proof. GelSight and TacTip produce depth maps and shear fields that are locally injective in (p, n) and (f, \mu), respectively, on the contact patch. The palm cameras observe the inter-digital space and provide a second viewpoint of the contact geometry. The injectivity of these maps on an open set follows from the spatial resolution of the sensors and the non-degeneracy of the contact patch. \square

B.6 Proposition (Restated)

Proposition B.4. Under the hypotheses of Lemmas B.2 and B.3, there exists an open set \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}} \subset \mathcal{X} such that for all x \in \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}},

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x) < \dim \mathcal{X}, \qquad \operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{dist}}(x) = \dim \mathcal{X}.

Proof. By Lemma B.2, \dim \mathcal{N}_{\text{ext}}(x) \geq 2 on \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}}, so

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x) \leq \dim \mathcal{X} - 2 < \dim \mathcal{X}.

For the distributed architecture, the additional rows H_{\text{tact}}, H_{\text{vis, palm}}, and H_{\text{int}} contribute independent constraints. Specifically, the tactile Jacobian \partial g_T / \partial c has rank \dim \mathcal{C} = 9k on \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}} by Lemma B.3, which exceeds the dimension of the null space of \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}. The internal forearm row \partial h_{\text{int}} / \partial (L, \tau) adds independent constraints on the tendon state, which in turn constrains (q, \tau) and thus the contact force balance. Combining the rows,

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{dist}}(x) = \operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x) + \Delta,

where \Delta \geq 2 on \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}}. Therefore \operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{dist}}(x) = \dim \mathcal{X} if the total number of independent rows is at least \dim \mathcal{X}, which holds for the sensor count in the V0.1 specification. \square

B.7 Rank Increase Lower Bound

Corollary B.5. Under the hypotheses of Proposition B.4,

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{dist}}(x) - \operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x) \geq \dim(\mathcal{C}) - \dim(\mathcal{W}_{\text{FT}}),

where \mathcal{W}_{\text{FT}} is the subspace of \mathcal{W} observable through the wrist F/T sensor alone. For k \geq 2 contacts and an occluded external camera, this lower bound is at least 2.

Proof. The external architecture observes at most the 6-dimensional wrench space through F/T, so

\dim(\mathcal{W}_{\text{FT}}) \leq 6.

The distributed architecture observes \dim(\mathcal{C}) = 9k contact dimensions through the tactile and palm channels. Subtracting the shared subspace, the rank increase is at least 9k - 6, which is \geq 2 for k \geq 1 and strictly larger for k \geq 2. \square

