Appendix C. Information-Theoretic Occlusion Analysis

C.1 Setup

Let M \in \{0, 1\} be the occlusion indicator. Under M = 0, the external visual channel provides no information about the object state:

I(w; z_{\text{vis, ext}} \mid M = 0, q) = 0.

This follows from the data processing inequality applied to the Markov chain

w \to \text{object surface} \to \text{occluded image} \to z_{\text{vis, ext}},

where the occluded image is independent of w given M = 0.

C.2 Theorem (Positive Information under Occlusion)

Theorem C.1. Under M = 0, the distributed architecture provides strictly positive information about the object state:

I(w; S_{\text{tact}}, S_{\text{vis, palm}} \mid M = 0, q, \tau) > 0.

Proof. The tactile measurement z_T = h_T(c, w) + v_T depends on w through the contact geometry and local object surface. Since the tactile sensor is in physical contact with the object, the mutual information

I(w; z_T \mid M = 0, q, \tau) > 0

holds whenever the contact patch contains local geometric information about w and the sensor noise covariance R_T \succ 0 is finite. The palm cameras similarly observe the inter-digital space, which is not occluded by the external view. Therefore

I(w; S_{\text{tact}}, S_{\text{vis, palm}} \mid M = 0, q, \tau) \geq I(w; z_T \mid M = 0, q, \tau) > 0.

\square

C.3 Information Gain

Definition C.2. The architectural information gain under occlusion is

\Delta I_{\text{occ}} = I(w; S_{\text{dist}} \mid M = 0) - I(w; S_{\text{ext}} \mid M = 0).

Corollary C.3. \Delta I_{\text{occ}} > 0 and is bounded below by

\Delta I_{\text{occ}} \geq I(w; z_T \mid M = 0) - 0 = I(w; z_T \mid M = 0).

C.4 Connection to Estimation Error

Proposition C.4. Let \hat{w} be the MMSE estimate from S_{\text{dist}} under occlusion. Then

\mathbb{E}[\|w - \hat{w}\|^2] \geq \frac{1}{2\pi e} \exp\left(\frac{2}{d_w} h(w \mid S_{\text{dist}}, M=0)\right),

and the reduction in conditional entropy relative to S_{\text{ext}} is

h(w \mid S_{\text{ext}}, M=0) - h(w \mid S_{\text{dist}}, M=0) = \Delta I_{\text{occ}}.

Proof. The first inequality is the entropy power inequality (Shannon lower bound). The second follows from the definition of mutual information:

I(w; S_{\text{dist}} \mid M=0) = h(w \mid M=0) - h(w \mid S_{\text{dist}}, M=0),

I(w; S_{\text{ext}} \mid M=0) = h(w \mid M=0) - h(w \mid S_{\text{ext}}, M=0).

Subtracting gives the claim. \square

C.5 Empirical Justification

Recent results on simultaneous tactile-visual perception report 85.5% average success over five challenging manipulation tasks, compared with 55.4% for vision-only policies, with the largest advantage in contact detection for thin and soft objects where visual occlusion is severe. This gap is consistent with \Delta I_{\text{occ}} > 0 and provides an empirical lower bound on the architectural information gain.

