Appendix D. Tendon Elasticity and Internal Perception

D.1 Hill-Type Actuation with Passive Compliance (H-PAC)

The tendon force–length relationship is modeled as

\tau(\Delta L, \dot{L}) = \tau_{\text{active}}(u) + \tau_{\text{passive}}(\Delta L) + \tau_{\text{damping}}(\dot{L}),

where u is the motor command and

\tau_{\text{passive}}(\Delta L) =
\begin{cases}
k_{\text{tendon}} \Delta L, & \Delta L > 0, \\
0, & \Delta L \leq 0,
\end{cases}

with k_{\text{tendon}} the tendon stiffness. The tendon elongation under restoring spring loading introduces configuration-dependent joint error:

q_{\text{actual}} = q_{\text{commanded}} + C^{-1}(q) \cdot \Delta L(\tau, q),

where C^T(q) \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n} is the coupling matrix mapping tendon tensions to joint torques. This is the H-PAC-like structure: actuation and passive compliance are modeled jointly, and the configuration-dependent term is not negligible.

D.2 Bowden-Cable Friction

For a tendon routed through a Bowden sheath with wrap angle \theta(s) and Coulomb friction coefficient \mu_c, the tension transmission follows the capstan equation

\tau_{\text{out}} = \tau_{\text{in}} \exp\left(-\mu_c \int_0^s \kappa(s') \, ds'\right),

where \kappa(s') is the local curvature. The configuration-dependent friction introduces hysteresis:

\tau_{\text{joint}}(t) =
\begin{cases}
\tau_{\text{in}} e^{-\mu_c \theta(q)}, & \dot{q} > 0, \\
\tau_{\text{in}} e^{+\mu_c \theta(q)}, & \dot{q} < 0.
\end{cases}

The internal forearm camera observes the deformation of the tendon/spring bundle directly, providing an estimate of \Delta L that compensates for this hysteresis.

D.3 Internal Perception Map

Definition D.1. The internal perception map is

\Psi : \mathbb{R}^m \times \mathcal{Q} \times \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathcal{D}, \qquad \delta = \Psi(\tau, q, L),

where \delta \in \mathcal{D} is the deformation field of the spring/tendon bundle observed by the forearm camera.

Linearization. Around (\tau_0, q_0, L_0),

\delta \approx \delta_0 + J_\Psi
\begin{bmatrix}
\Delta \tau \\
\Delta q \\
\Delta L
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad J_\Psi = \begin{bmatrix} \partial \Psi/\partial \tau & \partial \Psi/\partial q & \partial \Psi/\partial L \end{bmatrix}.

The camera observation is

z_P = \Phi(\delta) + v_P, \qquad \Phi: \mathcal{D} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_P}.

Linearizing,

z_P \approx \Phi(\delta_0) + J_\Phi J_\Psi
\begin{bmatrix}
\Delta \tau \\
\Delta q \\
\Delta L
\end{bmatrix}
+ v_P,
\qquad J_\Phi = \partial \Phi / \partial \delta.

D.4 Proposition (Internal Perception Constrains Tendon Length)

Proposition D.2. If \operatorname{rank}(J_\Phi J_\Psi) \geq m, then the internal camera observation z_P provides an independent constraint on \Delta L that is not available from motor current and encoders alone.

Proof. Motor current provides a noisy estimate of \tau_{\text{in}}, and encoders provide q. The map from (\tau_{\text{in}}, q) to \Delta L requires the friction model of Appendix D.2, which is uncertain. The internal camera observes \delta, which depends on (\Delta \tau, \Delta q, \Delta L). If the Jacobian has rank at least m, the map is locally invertible in \Delta L, providing a direct measurement that bypasses the friction uncertainty. \square

