Appendix E. Factor Graph Derivation and Message Passing

E.1 Factor Graph Definition

The factor graph \mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{F}) has variable nodes

\mathcal{V} = \{x_t, x_{t-1}, \dots, x_{t-L}\}

and factor nodes

\mathcal{F} = \mathcal{F}_{\text{dyn}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{prop}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{tact}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{vis}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{int}}.

The joint distribution factorizes as

p(\mathcal{V} \mid \mathcal{Z}) \propto \prod_{f_i \in \mathcal{F}} f_i(\mathcal{V}_i),

where \mathcal{V}_i is the set of variables connected to factor f_i.

E.2 Factor Definitions

Dynamics factor.

f_{\text{dyn}}(x_t, x_{t+1}) = \exp\left(-\tfrac{1}{2} \|x_{t+1} - f(x_t, u_t)\|_Q^2\right).

Proprioceptive factor.

f_{\text{prop}}(x_t) = \exp\left(-\tfrac{1}{2} \|z_Q - q_t\|_{R_Q^{-1}}^2\right).

Tactile factor.

f_{\text{tact}}(x_t) = \exp\left(-\tfrac{1}{2} \|z_T - h_T(c_t, w_t)\|_{R_T^{-1}}^2\right).

Visual factor.

f_{\text{vis}}(x_t) = \exp\left(-\tfrac{1}{2} \|z_V - h_V(w_t, q_t) \cdot M_{\text{vis}}\|_{R_V^{-1}}^2\right).

Internal factor.

f_{\text{int}}(x_t) = \exp\left(-\tfrac{1}{2} \|z_P - \Phi \circ \Psi(\tau_t, q_t, L_t)\|_{R_P^{-1}}^2\right).

E.3 MAP Estimation as Nonlinear Least Squares

The MAP estimate is

\hat{\mathcal{V}} = \arg\min_{\mathcal{V}} \sum_{f_i \in \mathcal{F}} \|r_i(\mathcal{V}_i)\|_{R_i^{-1}}^2,

where r_i is the residual of factor f_i. This is a nonlinear least-squares problem solved by Gauss–Newton:

\mathcal{V}^{(k+1)} = \mathcal{V}^{(k)} - \left(J^T R^{-1} J\right)^{-1} J^T R^{-1} r(\mathcal{V}^{(k)}),

with J the stacked Jacobian of all residuals.

E.4 Sparse Structure and Marginalization

The sparsity pattern of J^T R^{-1} J reflects the mechanical topology. The wrist separator node induces a block-arrow structure:

J^T R^{-1} J =
\begin{bmatrix}
\Lambda_A & 0 & \Lambda_{A,W} \\
0 & \Lambda_B & \Lambda_{B,W} \\
\Lambda_{A,W}^T & \Lambda_{B,W}^T & \Lambda_W
\end{bmatrix},

where \Lambda_A is the hand block, \Lambda_B the forearm block, and \Lambda_W the wrist coupling. Marginalizing the wrist node yields

\Lambda_A' = \Lambda_A - \Lambda_{A,W} \Lambda_W^{-1} \Lambda_{A,W}^T,

\Lambda_B' = \Lambda_B - \Lambda_{B,W} \Lambda_W^{-1} \Lambda_{B,W}^T.

This is the algebraic form of the conditional independence statement in Section 2.2.

E.5 Message Passing

For Gaussian factors, sum-product message passing is equivalent to the Gauss–Newton update. The message from factor f_i to variable x_j is

m_{f_i \to x_j}(x_j) \propto \exp\left(-\tfrac{1}{2} \|x_j - \mu_{i \to j}\|_{\Sigma_{i \to j}^{-1}}^2\right),

with

\Sigma_{i \to j} = \left(J_{ij}^T R_i^{-1} J_{ij}\right)^{-1}, \qquad \mu_{i \to j} = \Sigma_{i \to j} J_{ij}^T R_i^{-1} (z_i - r_i^{(0)}).

The belief at variable x_j is the product of incoming messages:

b(x_j) \propto \prod_{f_i \in \text{ne}(x_j)} m_{f_i \to x_j}(x_j).

