Appendix F. Contact Parameter Identifiability

F.1 Identifiability Definition

Definition F.1. The contact state c = (p, n, f, \mu) is identifiable from measurements (z_T, z_V, z_Q) if the map

(c, w) \mapsto (z_T, z_V, z_Q)

is injective on the domain of interest.

F.2 Theorem (Sufficient Conditions for Identifiability)

Theorem F.2. The contact state c = (p, n, f, \mu) is identifiable if:

1. the tactile sensor has sufficient spatial resolution to resolve the pressure map z_T at the contact patch;
2. the visual system observes the contact from at least two non-collinear viewpoints;
3. the tactile sensor is sensitive to shear (for \mu);
4. the finger F/T sensor provides tangential force measurements.

Proof. Condition 1 ensures that (p, n) are recoverable from the pressure map via photometric stereo or marker tracking. Condition 2 ensures that the local object geometry w is constrained from multiple viewpoints, removing the depth ambiguity of a single view. Condition 3 ensures that the tangential displacement field is observed, from which \mu is recoverable via the relation

f_t = \mu f_n

at the slip boundary. Condition 4 provides an independent measurement of the tangential force, which disambiguates \mu from f_n when the normal force is uncertain. Together, these conditions make the map injective. \square

F.3 Heterogeneous Sensor Configuration

The GelSight360 sensor satisfies conditions 1 and 2 (omnidirectional depth maps from photometric stereo). The TacTip sensor satisfies condition 3 (shear via lateral marker displacement). The finger F/T sensor satisfies condition 4. Therefore the heterogeneous configuration — GelSight on thumb and index, TacTip on middle, ring, and little — satisfies the sufficient conditions of Theorem F.2.

F.4 Rank Condition

Corollary F.3. Under the conditions of Theorem F.2,

\operatorname{rank}\left(\frac{\partial (z_T, z_V, z_Q)}{\partial (c, w)}\right) = \dim(\mathcal{C}) + \dim(\mathcal{W}).

Proof. Injectivity of a differentiable map on an open set implies full column rank of its Jacobian. \square

