Appendix G. Sensor Degradation and Thermal Noise Models

G.1 Wear Model

Let n_g be the cumulative number of grasps. The GelSight signal strength decays as

S(n_g) = S_0 \exp(-\lambda_w n_g),

with \lambda_w the wear rate. The noise covariance grows as

R_T(n_g) = R_T^{(0)} \left(1 + \alpha_w n_g\right),

where \alpha_w is the wear coefficient. Empirical data from GelSlim indicate signal strength degradation after approximately 1700 grasps and restoration via online brightness and contrast adjustment over 3300 grasps, implying \lambda_w \approx 1/1700 and a calibration update rate of order 1/1000 grasps.

G.2 Thermal Model

The thermal noise covariance is modeled as

R_i(T) = R_i^{(0)} \left(1 + \beta_T (T - T_0)^2\right),

where T is the sensor temperature and T_0 the calibration temperature. For camera-based tactile sensors, thermal expansion of the elastomer and drift in the illumination source contribute to \beta_T. The total covariance is

R_i(t, T) = R_i^{(0)} + \Sigma_{\text{wear}}(n_g) + \Sigma_{\text{thermal}}(T).

G.3 Calibration Update

Definition G.1. The calibration update at time t is

\hat{R}_i(t) = \arg\min_{R \succ 0} \sum_{s=t-K}^{t} \|z_i(s) - h_i(\hat{x}_s)\|_R^2 + \gamma \|\hat{R}_i(t-1) - R\|_F^2,

with \gamma the regularization weight. This is a recursive covariance estimation that tracks wear and thermal drift.

