The underactuation per finger (3 joints, 1 tendon, 1 return spring) introduces a 2-dimensional internal null space per finger, which is observed by the internal forearm camera through the tendon length and tension.

H.3 Null Space and Internal Perception

Proposition H.1. The internal forearm camera reduces the dimension of the unobservable subspace by at least the rank of the finger coupling matrix.

Proof. The finger null space is

\mathcal{N}_{\text{finger}} = \ker C_{\text{finger}}^T(q),

with \dim \mathcal{N}_{\text{finger}} = 3 - \operatorname{rank} C_{\text{finger}}^T = 2. The internal camera observes \Delta L, which depends on the joint configuration through

\Delta L = \sum_i r_i(q) \Delta q_i.

This provides one constraint per tendon, reducing the null space dimension by 1 per finger. The remaining null space is resolved by the tactile and palm channels. \square

